-### Parameters and Edge Cases
-*   **Packet Size:** The ISPC intersection routine processes ray packets of 4, 8, or 16 rays. Due to the small size of voxels, the number of active rays per intersection is often small, which may lead to underutilization of SIMD units [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Rectilinear vs. Regular:** For general rectilinear volumes, the voxel abstraction must account for varying height and depth, not just width [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Precision:** The method relies on the accuracy of the implicit intersection routine. Degenerate voxels (zero width/height/depth) should be filtered out during active voxel identification.
+**Parameters:**
+*   **Isovalue:** The scalar value defining the isosurface.
+*   **Ray Packet Size:** 4, 8, or 16 rays, depending on the hardware and ray tracer configuration [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Precision:** The 64-bit encoding of voxel coordinates must be sufficient to uniquely identify voxels in the volume. If the volume exceeds the addressable space of the encoding scheme, collisions may occur [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Refined Regions:** Fixed-step sample-based methods fail in refined regions because the step size is too large to capture the surface. This hybrid method avoids this by using implicit intersection within each voxel, ensuring detail is preserved regardless of voxel size [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Empty Voxels:** Voxels that do not contain the isovalue are excluded from the BVH, reducing memory overhead and traversal cost [S1, unofficial].
 
